# vike-desktop — the observe-only GUI client, as a container reachable from any host OS.
#
# ⚠ The FILENAME still says `thin`, and so do the `build-thin` verb and the published `vike-thin`
# tag, after the crate's `thin` FEATURE was deleted. Nothing here selects a configuration any more:
# `vike-desktop` has exactly one, and it is the observe-only client this file was always built from.
# The names are kept because the image tag is user-facing and paired with the backend image's
# stream; renaming them is a separate decision.
#
# The SECOND image. `deploy/docker/Dockerfile` is the backend daemon; this is the client that talks
# to it. Two images, one for each half, which is what an operator asked for and what the split-plane
# architecture already is: `vike-desktop --observe <ADDR>` links no venue bridge and no DataFusion,
# so it renders what a remote daemon sends and holds no trading core of its own.
#
# ## ⚠ Why this needs a display stack, and why that is not a workaround
#
# `vike-desktop` is egui/wgpu — it draws with a GPU API. A container has no display server and, on
# Windows and macOS, no path to the host's GPU at all. The usual conclusion is "ship the GUI as a
# native binary", and that remains the fastest option for anyone who can run one.
#
# But `0026`'s "there is no version of containerising the GUI worth the sentence" was written about
# GPU passthrough, and there is a second path it did not weigh: **this application already renders
# with no GPU whatsoever, every PR.** `scripts/ci_feature_suite.sh`'s `png-export` lane runs both GUI
# harnesses on **Mesa lavapipe**, a pure software rasteriser, on GPU-less runners, and reads its own
# pixels back against `vike_ui_theme::pixel_liveness`'s floors. Software rendering is not a
# theoretical fallback here; it is a gated, exercised configuration.
#
# So this image renders into a virtual framebuffer with lavapipe and exports the result over VNC.
# It works identically on Linux, Windows and macOS hosts because nothing in it touches host
# hardware. What it costs is real and belongs on the label: **lavapipe is a CPU rasteriser**, so a
# live chart will not feel like the native binary. Measure before promising it to anyone.
#
# ## Route (a), same as the server image
#
# The binary is COPIED in, not compiled here — `crates/vike-buildinfo`'s `build.rs` resolves
# `GIT_SHA` from real git paths, so a build inside a context with no `.git` produces a client that
# cannot say which commit it is. Build it with:
#
#     cargo build --release -p vike-desktop
#
#   docker build -f deploy/docker/Dockerfile.thin -t vike-thin:<version> .

# Same digest-pinned base as the backend image, deliberately: one base to patch, one CVE stream.
FROM ubuntu:24.04@sha256:33ceb71981b602c1a7443a53469e4dba065f7503eab3078a2d7a57a2ab987517

# ── the display stack ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# xvfb          a virtual framebuffer — the display server this image has no host equivalent for
# mesa-vulkan-drivers   carries lavapipe (`lvp_icd`), the software Vulkan ICD wgpu selects
# x11vnc        exports that framebuffer as VNC
# novnc/websockify   the same stream over a browser, so a client needs no VNC application
# fonts-dejavu-core  egui renders NOTHING legible without a font on the box; omitting it produces
#                    a window that draws and shows no text, which reads as a broken build
# ca-certificates    the client speaks to the node over the network like any other consumer
#
# ⚠ The `libX*` line is winit's, not egui's, and every entry there was found by a CRASH rather than
# by reading a manifest. winit dlopen()s them at window creation, so a missing one is not a build
# error and not a startup error — it is a runtime `XNotSupported(LibraryOpenError)` after the app has
# already logged its version and settings, which reads like an application fault rather than a
# packaging one. `libXcursor` was the first; the rest are its usual companions.
RUN apt-get update \
 && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
      xvfb x11vnc novnc websockify \
      mesa-vulkan-drivers libvulkan1 libgl1-mesa-dri \
      libxcursor1 libxrandr2 libxi6 libxinerama1 libxext6 libxrender1 libxfixes3 \
      libxkbcommon-x11-0 libxcb-icccm4 libxcb-image0 libxcb-keysyms1 libxcb-randr0 \
      libxcb-render-util0 libxcb-shape0 libxcb-xinerama0 libxcb-xfixes0 \
      fonts-dejavu-core ca-certificates \
 && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/* \
 && useradd --uid 10001 --user-group --create-home --shell /usr/sbin/nologin vike \
 # Xvfb creates this itself only as root. This image runs as uid 10001, so it must exist first —
 # 1777 like any /tmp subdirectory. Without it Xvfb falls back to an abstract socket and the
 # readiness probe (which watches for the socket FILE) never fires.
 && mkdir -p /tmp/.X11-unix && chmod 1777 /tmp/.X11-unix \
 # vike-log's last-resort log directory is `<exe_dir>/logs`, which here is /opt/vike/bin/logs — owned
 # by root and not writable by the runtime user, so every start printed a permission error before
 # disabling the file layer. Give it a writable home instead of letting it fail into a warning.
 && mkdir -p /var/log/vike && chown 10001:10001 /var/log/vike \
 # ── drop the GPU Vulkan drivers this image can never load ──────────────────────────────────────
 # `mesa-vulkan-drivers` ships EIGHT ICDs and `VK_ICD_FILENAMES` below pins exactly one — lavapipe.
 # The other seven are for hardware a container with no device passthrough cannot reach, so they are
 # dead weight that a `docker pull` still pays for. MEASURED on the 0.1.15 image: intel 21 MB,
 # intel_hasvk 17, nouveau 16, radeon 15, asahi 12 — 81 MB of an image whose apt layer was 626 MB.
 #
 # ⚠ `libvulkan_lvp.so` and its `lvp_icd.json` are the ONLY pair kept, and the manifests go with the
 # libraries: an ICD json naming a missing .so makes the loader log a failure for every enumeration.
 # ⚠ This does NOT touch `libLLVM.so.20.1` (137 MB), which is the single largest file in the image
 # and is not removable — lavapipe JIT-compiles shaders through it. Software rendering costs that.
 && for d in intel intel_hasvk nouveau radeon asahi virtio gfxstream; do \
      rm -f "/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libvulkan_${d}.so" \
            "/usr/share/vulkan/icd.d/${d}_icd.json" \
            "/usr/share/vulkan/icd.d/${d}_vk_icd.json"; \
    done \
 && [ -f /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libvulkan_lvp.so ] || { echo "lavapipe was removed — the image cannot render" >&2; exit 1; }

COPY target/release/vike-desktop    /opt/vike/bin/vike-desktop
COPY deploy/docker/entrypoint-thin.sh /opt/vike/entrypoint-thin.sh
RUN chmod 0755 /opt/vike/bin/vike-desktop /opt/vike/entrypoint-thin.sh

# ⚠ ZERO CLICKS. noVNC's `vnc.html` opens a DISCONNECTED page with a Connect button, so
# `http://localhost:6080` showed a blank panel and read as "the client is broken" — reported
# exactly that way. websockify serves `/` from this directory, so an `index.html` that carries the
# autoconnect parameters is the whole fix: the URL a person types now shows the GUI.
#
# `resize=scale` because the container's virtual screen is a fixed size and a browser window is
# not; without it the GUI is cropped rather than fitted, which is the second thing that reads as
# broken. `reconnect=true` because the app restarts faster than a person re-reads the URL.
RUN printf '%s\n' \
      '<!doctype html><meta charset="utf-8"><title>vike — thin client</title>' \
      '<meta http-equiv="refresh" content="0; url=vnc.html?autoconnect=true&resize=scale&reconnect=true">' \
      '<p>Opening the vike thin client… <a href="vnc.html?autoconnect=true&resize=scale&reconnect=true">continue</a></p>' \
      > /usr/share/novnc/index.html

# ⚠ Force the SOFTWARE ICD explicitly rather than letting wgpu enumerate. A host that DOES expose a
# GPU (Linux with `--device /dev/dri`) would otherwise be selected silently, so the same image would
# render two different ways depending on the host and a bug would reproduce on one and not the
# other. Pinning it makes the configuration the same everywhere; a GPU-passthrough variant should be
# a deliberate second tag, not an accident of the host.
# ⚠ `lvp_icd.json`, with NO architecture suffix. Ubuntu 24.04's mesa-vulkan-drivers ships it under
# that name; several distributions and most tutorials use `lvp_icd.x86_64.json`, which does not
# exist here. Pointing at the wrong path does not error — the loader simply finds no ICD, so Vulkan
# reports no adapter and wgpu fails much later with `Failed to create surface for any enabled
# backend`, which reads as a surface problem rather than a missing driver.
ENV VK_ICD_FILENAMES=/usr/share/vulkan/icd.d/lvp_icd.json \
    LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1 \
    WGPU_BACKEND=vulkan \
    DISPLAY=:99 \
    VIKE_LOG_DIR=/var/log/vike \
    VIKE_THIN_GEOMETRY=1600x900x24

# 5900 = VNC, 6080 = noVNC in a browser. Publish ONLY to loopback and tunnel, exactly as the node
# port is treated: this stream carries a live trading view and VNC's own auth is not a substitute.
EXPOSE 5900 6080
USER 10001:10001
WORKDIR /home/vike
ENTRYPOINT ["/opt/vike/entrypoint-thin.sh"]
